  "asr_text": "First thing every morning, I see the faces of the kids who come to the nurse's office. I'm sleepy, they say. My head hurts, they say. The other day, a phone screen was glowing beside the pillow of a student resting on the bed. When I hear they were up until dawn, I wonder what I should say. I also keep thinking about a student who stopped coming the day after their phone was taken away at school. If they don't come, does that mean they aren't struggling? A teacher asked me, if we ban phones, will fewer kids come to the nurse's office? I couldn't answer. If the school makes phones off-limits, even during breaks, won't it feel it has dealt with the problem? That really scares me. I don't take phones away in the nurse's office. I want to keep a place where students can talk about what happened at night. After phones are off-limits, who will listen to that child?",
